# Exact Nearest-Neighbour in O(1): What Actually Works

## The Theoretical Reality

For truly arbitrary high-dimensional points (continuous coordinates, unbounded dimension), exact nearest-neighbour in O(1) per query is **impossible** with subexponential space. This is a fundamental result in computational geometry.

However, O(1) **is** achievable in specific scenarios. Here are the main approaches, from most to least restrictive:

| Approach | Query Time | Restriction |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| Precomputation | **O(1)** | Query set must be fixed & known in advance |
| Grid lookup | **O(1)** | Integer coordinates in `[0, K)^d` (space is `O(K^d)`) |
| k-d tree | O(log n) avg | None (but degrades in high dimensions) |
| LSH | O(1) approx | Approximate only |

Let me show you all of them in C++.

---

## Approach 1: Precomputation (True O(1))

If you know all queries in advance, just precompute answers into a hash table.

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>

using Point = std::vector<double>;

inline double dist2(const Point& a, const Point& b) {
    double s = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
        double d = a[i] - b[i];
        s += d * d;
    }
    return s;
}

class PrecomputedNN {
    std::unordered_map<int, int> answer_;  // query_id -> data_id

public:
    // Build: O(|queries| * n * d)
    void build(const std::vector<Point>& data,
               const std::vector<Point>& queries) {
        answer_.reserve(queries.size());
        for (size_t q = 0; q < queries.size(); ++q) {
            int best = 0;
            double best_d2 = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
            for (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); ++i) {
                double d2 = dist2(queries[q], data[i]);
                if (d2 < best_d2) { best_d2 = d2; best = static_cast<int>(i); }
            }
            answer_[static_cast<int>(q)] = best;
        }
    }

    // Query: O(1) — just a hash lookup
    int query(int q) const { return answer_.at(q); }
};
```

---

## Approach 2: Grid Lookup (True O(1, but exponential space)

If coordinates are integers in `[0, K)^d`, discretize space into a grid. Each cell stores its nearest data point.

```cpp
class GridNN {
    int K_, d_;
    std::vector<int> cell_;  // size K^d

    int index(const Point& p) const {
        int idx = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < d_; ++i)
            idx = idx * K_ + static_cast<int>(p[i]);  // p[i] must be in [0, K)
        return idx;
    }

public:
    GridNN(int K, int d) : K_(K), d_(d) {
        size_t total = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < d_; ++i) total *= K_;
        cell_.assign(total, -1);
    }

    // Build: O(K^d * n * d) — exponential in d!
    void build(const std::vector<Point>& data) {
        for (size_t idx = 0; idx < cell_.size(); ++idx) {
            // Decode cell index -> point at cell center
            Point p(d_);
            size_t tmp = idx;
            for (int i = d_ - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
                p[i] = static_cast<double>(tmp % K_);
                tmp /= K_;
            }
            int best = 0;
            double best_d2 = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
            for (size_t i = 0; i < data.size(); ++i) {
                double d2 = dist2(p, data[i]);
                if (d2 < best_d2) { best_d2 = d2; best = static_cast<int>(i); }
            }
            cell_[idx] = best;
        }
    }

    // Query: O(1)
    int query(const Point& p) const { return cell_[index(p)]; }
};
```

⚠️ **Caveat:** `K^d` blows up fast. With `K=100, d=10` you have `10^20` cells. Only practical for `d ≤ 3`.

---

## Approach 3: k-d Tree (O(log n) — the practical general solution)

For arbitrary points with no preprocessing constraints, a k-d tree is the standard tool. It cycles through dimensions, splitting on the median each time.

```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <algorithm>

struct KDNode {
    Point pt;
    int idx, dim;
    std::unique_ptr<KDNode> left, right;
    KDNode(const Point& p, int i, int d) : pt(p), idx(i), dim(d) {}
};

class KDTree {
    std::unique_ptr<KDNode> root_;

    std::unique_ptr<KDNode> build(std::vector<Point>& pts,
                                  std::vector<int>& ids, int depth) {
        if (ids.empty()) return nullptr;
        int d = static_cast<int>(pts[0].size());
        int axis = depth % d;

        size_t mid = ids.size() / 2;
        std::nth_element(ids.begin(), ids.begin() + mid, ids.end(),
            [&](int a, int b) { return pts[a][axis] < pts[b][axis]; });

        auto node = std::make_unique<KDNode>(pts[ids[mid]], ids[mid], axis);
        std::vector<int> L(ids.begin(), ids.begin() + mid);
        std::vector<int> R(ids.begin() + mid + 1, ids.end());
        node->left  = build(pts, L, depth + 1);
        node->right = build(pts, R, depth + 1);
        return node;
    }

    void nn(const KDNode* node, const Point& target,
            int& best_idx, double& best_d2) const {
        if (!node) return;
        double d = dist2(target, node->pt);
        if (d < best_d2) { best_d2 = d; best_idx = node->idx; }

        double diff = target[node->dim] - node->pt[node->dim];
        const KDNode* first  = diff < 0 ? node->left.get() : node->right.get();
        const KDNode* second = diff < 0 ? node->right.get() : node->left.get();

        nn(first, target, best_idx, best_d2);
        if (diff * diff < best_d2)              // prune: can the other side be closer?
            nn(second, target, best_idx, best_d2);
    }

public:
    explicit KDTree(const std::vector<Point>& points) {
        std::vector<Point> pts = points;
        std::vector<int> ids(points.size());
        for (size_t i = 0; i < ids.size(); ++i) ids[i] = static_cast<int>(i);
        root_ = build(pts, ids, 0);
    }

    // Query: O(log n) average, O(n) worst case in high dimensions
    int nearest(const Point& target) const {
        int best = -1;
        double best_d2 = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
        nn(root_.get(), target, best, best_d2);
        return best;
    }
};
```

⚠️ **Caveat:** In high dimensions (say `d > 20`), the pruning rarely helps and you get O(n) per query — the *curse of dimensionality*.

---

## Approach 4: LSH (Approximate, Sublinear)

When you need high-dimensional approximate NN, **Locality-Sensitive Hashing** is the standard tool. The idea: hash points so that nearby points collide with high probability. You only examine points in the same bucket as the query.

```cpp
#include <functional>

// Simplified LSH for Euclidean distance (using random projections)
class LSH {
    int d_, num_tables_, num_hashes_;
    std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<int>>> buckets_;  // [table][hash] -> point ids
    std::vector<std::function<int(const Point&)>> hash_fns_;
    std::vector<Point> data_;

public:
    LSH(int d, int num_tables = 10, int num_hashes = 4)
        : d_(d), num_tables_(num_tables), num_hashes_(num_hashes) {
        // In practice, use random hyperplane hashes: h(p) = sign(w . p + b)
        // See https://github.com/FALCONN-LIB/FALCONN for a real implementation
    }
    // ... build() and query() omitted — this is a sketch
};
```

For a real implementation, use a library like **FAISS**, **FLANN**, or **FALCONN**.

---

## Putting It All Together — A Working Demo

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <random>
#include <chrono>

int main() {
    std::mt19937 rng(42);
    const int n = 1000, d = 8, nq = 100;

    std::uniform_real_distribution<double> U(0.0, 100.0);
    std::vector<Point> data(n, Point(d));
    std::vector<Point> queries(nq, Point(d));
    for (int i = 0; i < n;  ++i) for (int j = 0; j < d; ++j) data[i][j]     = U(rng);
    for (int i = 0; i < nq; ++i) for (int j = 0; j < d; ++j) queries[i][j]  = U(rng);

    // --- Precomputation: O(1) per query ---
    PrecomputedNN pnn;
    auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    pnn.build(data, queries);
    auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    int idx1 = pnn.query(0);  // O(1) hash lookup
    auto t2 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    std::cout << "Precomputed: build="
              << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(t1-t0).count() << "us"
              << "  query="
              << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t2-t1).count() << "ns\n";

    // --- k-d tree: O(log n) per query ---
    KDTree tree(data);
    auto t3 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    int idx2 = tree.nearest(queries[0]);
    auto t4 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    std::cout << "k-d tree:    query="
              << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t4-t3).count() << "ns\n";

    // Both should agree
    std::cout << "Agreement: " << (idx1 == idx2 ? "YES" : "NO") << "\n";
}
```

---

## TL;DR — Which Should You Use?

| Your situation | Use |
|----------------|-----|
| Queries known in advance | **Precomputation** (true O(1)) |
| Integer coords, low dimension | **Grid** (true O(1)) |
| General exact NN, moderate dimension | **k-d tree** (O(log n)) |
| High dimension, approximation OK | **LSH / FAISS / FLANN** |

The honest truth: there is no magic bullet. Exact O(1) NN for arbitrary high-dimensional points with subexponential space is a proven impossibility. The approaches above let you pick the tradeoff that fits your problem.

